<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <RootNamespace>Nexus.ProductCore.Core</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Nexus.ProductCore.Core</AssemblyName>
    <IsPackable>true</IsPackable>
    <PackageId>Nexus.ProductCore.Core</PackageId>
    <Authors>PRT</Authors>
    <Description>Nexus V2 - Layer 06 Product Core - Nexus.ProductCore.Core</Description>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    W8D-R4 TASK 2. Layer 06 SHARED PLATFORM - the canonical DevelopmentControl
    reader/writer/locking implementation, moved here from Forge per Owner decision R4-01.

    ZERO PackageReferences and ZERO ProjectReferences, deliberately and by measurement.

    This is the BOOTSTRAP_SAFE property that LAYER_MODEL.md section 06 requires: "zero-IO, no
    dependency on this layer's own product-specific types - Forge may consume it even when
    Nexus's runtime/Shared Platform is down". It is not asserted here, it is constructed:
    the moved sources use only BCL namespaces (System.IO.Compression, System.Xml.Linq,
    System.Security.Cryptography, System.Text.Json, System.Runtime.InteropServices) and
    nothing else. Measured on the source lineage before the move.

    In particular there is NO ClosedXML reference and no OOXML SDK reference. The OOXML
    reading is done with ZipArchive + XDocument over the BCL. The R4-01 mechanisms therefore
    need no RUNTIME_DEPENDENT split, and this assembly stays consumable during a Nexus
    recovery.

    Adding any PackageReference or ProjectReference to this file is an architectural change,
    not a build tweak: it is the difference between an assembly Forge can always consume and
    one it cannot. See W8DR4_COMPONENT_OWNERSHIP.md.
  -->

</Project>
